Source-free domain adaptation (SFDA) has garnered significant attention recently, yet most SFDA techniques necessitate access to the original source data, which limits their practical utility. To address this limitation, this paper introduces SSL-T3, a novel framework designed for SFDA that utilizes unlabeled data from both the source and target domains while eliminating the need for the source. SSL-T3 integrates two core components: a semi-supervised theory transfer module that aligns pseudo-label distributions across source and target sources, and a self-distillation module that enhances discriminative feature representation learning. Extensive evaluations on three benchmark datasets demonstrate the effectiveness of SSL-T3, revealing superior performance compared to state-of-the-art SFDA methods. The source code is accessible at https://github.com/Chenchenchenchen/SSL-T3. Furthermore, additional results and video materials can be found at https://chenchenchenchen.github.io/. Finally, please cite our work if you find it helpful. Thank you.
